1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,061
Friday. 4:15 PM.

2
00:00:03,101 --> 00:00:05,596
School lets out early because of the snow.

3
00:00:05,636 --> 00:00:07,044
Nobody minds.

4
00:00:07,084 --> 00:00:09,401
It's the first real snow of the year,

5
00:00:09,441 --> 00:00:10,972
and it's beautiful.

6
00:00:11,012 --> 00:00:13,631
Big, slow flakes, the kind that stick

7
00:00:13,671 --> 00:00:15,364
to your eyelashes.

8
00:00:15,404 --> 00:00:17,454
By dinner, the car is a white hill

9
00:00:17,494 --> 00:00:18,921
in the driveway.

10
00:00:18,961 --> 00:00:20,837
By midnight, you can't see the house

11
00:00:20,877 --> 00:00:22,338
across the street.

12
00:00:22,378 --> 00:00:24,873
By morning, the snow is up to the windows.

13
00:00:24,913 --> 00:00:26,603
And it's still falling.

14
00:00:27,052 --> 00:00:29,501
The radio says it will stop tomorrow.

15
00:00:29,541 --> 00:00:31,060
It doesn't stop tomorrow.

16
00:00:31,100 --> 00:00:32,759
It doesn't stop that week.

17
00:00:32,799 --> 00:00:34,876
It snows for forty days.

18
00:00:34,916 --> 00:00:36,436
Not just in your town.

19
00:00:36,476 --> 00:00:38,089
Across the whole country.

20
00:00:38,129 --> 00:00:39,350
No plows.

21
00:00:39,390 --> 00:00:40,844
No deliveries.

22
00:00:40,884 --> 00:00:42,292
No way out.

23
00:00:42,332 --> 00:00:44,533
On the first morning, there's a card taped

24
00:00:44,573 --> 00:00:46,870
to every front door in town.

25
00:00:46,910 --> 00:00:48,549
White plastic.

26
00:00:48,589 --> 00:00:50,778
Twenty rules, printed in small black

27
00:00:50,818 --> 00:00:51,920
letters.

28
00:00:51,960 --> 00:00:53,898
Nobody knows who put them there.

29
00:00:53,938 --> 00:00:55,349
The people who followed them saw

30
00:00:55,389 --> 00:00:56,386
the spring.

31
00:00:56,426 --> 00:00:57,930
And you're going to learn them the same

32
00:00:57,970 --> 00:00:59,339
way you did.

33
00:00:59,379 --> 00:01:00,759
One day at a time.

34
00:01:00,799 --> 00:01:03,035
Listen to the end, because rule twenty

35
00:01:03,075 --> 00:01:04,780
is the one that matters.

36
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:07,531
Day one. The cards.

37
00:01:07,832 --> 00:01:10,253
On the first day, nobody takes the card

38
00:01:10,293 --> 00:01:11,673
seriously.

39
00:01:11,713 --> 00:01:13,577
Your dad laughs and pins it to the fridge

40
00:01:13,617 --> 00:01:14,812
with a magnet.

41
00:01:14,852 --> 00:01:16,449
Then he drives to the hospital for his

42
00:01:16,489 --> 00:01:17,625
night shift.

43
00:01:17,665 --> 00:01:19,413
It's the last time you see him for forty

44
00:01:19,453 --> 00:01:20,609
days.

45
00:01:21,259 --> 00:01:23,820
That night, it's just you, your little

46
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:26,587
sister Maya, and the snow.

47
00:01:26,627 --> 00:01:28,425
The power is still on.

48
00:01:28,465 --> 00:01:31,025
The TV says it's just a storm system,

49
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:32,848
nothing unusual.

50
00:01:33,548 --> 00:01:34,716
Rule one.

51
00:01:34,756 --> 00:01:37,142
On the first day, fill everything you have

52
00:01:37,182 --> 00:01:38,226
with water.

53
00:01:38,266 --> 00:01:39,488
The bathtub.

54
00:01:39,528 --> 00:01:40,935
Every pot.

55
00:01:40,975 --> 00:01:42,290
Every bottle.

56
00:01:42,330 --> 00:01:43,985
When it gets cold enough, the pipes

57
00:01:44,025 --> 00:01:46,775
freeze, and the tap just stops.

58
00:01:46,815 --> 00:01:48,900
Open the cabinet doors under the sinks,

59
00:01:48,940 --> 00:01:51,446
and let the taps drip a little at night.

60
00:01:51,486 --> 00:01:53,745
Moving water freezes slower than still

61
00:01:53,785 --> 00:01:54,910
water.

62
00:01:54,950 --> 00:01:56,702
Maya thinks it's a game.

63
00:01:56,742 --> 00:01:58,571
She fills her rain boots.

64
00:01:59,271 --> 00:02:00,439
Rule two.

65
00:02:00,479 --> 00:02:02,834
Pick one room, and live in it.

66
00:02:02,874 --> 00:02:04,766
You can't heat a whole house.

67
00:02:04,806 --> 00:02:06,507
So you pick the living room, because

68
00:02:06,547 --> 00:02:07,951
it has the fireplace.

69
00:02:07,991 --> 00:02:09,510
Towels under the doors.

70
00:02:09,550 --> 00:02:11,070
Blankets over the windows.

71
00:02:11,110 --> 00:02:12,908
Mattresses on the floor.

72
00:02:12,948 --> 00:02:14,533
Every other room in the house becomes

73
00:02:14,573 --> 00:02:17,533
a place you visit quickly, wearing a coat.

74
00:02:17,573 --> 00:02:19,367
A closed door keeps the heat where

75
00:02:19,407 --> 00:02:20,888
you are.

76
00:02:21,588 --> 00:02:22,756
Rule three.

77
00:02:22,796 --> 00:02:25,059
Never let the snow bury the vents.

78
00:02:25,099 --> 00:02:28,476
The furnace, the dryer, the water heater.

79
00:02:28,516 --> 00:02:29,869
They all breathe through pipes

80
00:02:29,909 --> 00:02:31,522
in the outside wall.

81
00:02:31,562 --> 00:02:33,751
When the snow covers them, the fumes come

82
00:02:33,791 --> 00:02:35,171
back inside.

83
00:02:35,211 --> 00:02:36,406
You can't see it.

84
00:02:36,446 --> 00:02:37,872
You can't smell it.

85
00:02:37,912 --> 00:02:39,525
You just get very tired.

86
00:02:39,565 --> 00:02:42,253
If anyone gets a headache, feels dizzy,

87
00:02:42,293 --> 00:02:44,273
or sick to their stomach all at the same

88
00:02:44,313 --> 00:02:47,075
time, get fresh air immediately.

89
00:02:47,115 --> 00:02:49,142
Twice a day, you dig out the vent

90
00:02:49,182 --> 00:02:50,790
on the side of the house with a kitchen

91
00:02:50,830 --> 00:02:51,793
pot.

92
00:02:51,833 --> 00:02:53,395
And there's something else on the card,

93
00:02:53,435 --> 00:02:56,278
under rule three, in smaller letters.

94
00:02:56,318 --> 00:02:58,078
It says, the snow is not the only thing

95
00:02:58,118 --> 00:02:59,277
that's cold.

96
00:02:59,317 --> 00:03:02,075
Day three. The cold.

97
00:03:02,375 --> 00:03:05,056
On the third day, the power goes out.

98
00:03:05,096 --> 00:03:06,643
The furnace stops.

99
00:03:06,683 --> 00:03:08,373
The silence is so complete that you can

100
00:03:08,413 --> 00:03:10,431
hear the snow landing on the roof.

101
00:03:10,471 --> 00:03:13,241
Across the street, Mr. Pratt drags his gas

102
00:03:13,281 --> 00:03:15,319
grill into his garage.

103
00:03:16,019 --> 00:03:17,605
Rule four.

104
00:03:17,645 --> 00:03:19,672
Never use a grill, a generator,

105
00:03:19,712 --> 00:03:22,090
or a camping stove inside.

106
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:23,371
Not in the house.

107
00:03:23,411 --> 00:03:24,885
Not in the garage.

108
00:03:24,925 --> 00:03:26,630
Not even with the door open.

109
00:03:26,670 --> 00:03:29,207
They make carbon monoxide, and in a closed

110
00:03:29,247 --> 00:03:31,506
space, it builds up faster than you'd ever

111
00:03:31,546 --> 00:03:32,648
believe.

112
00:03:32,688 --> 00:03:34,703
Mr. Pratt's garage light stays on all

113
00:03:34,743 --> 00:03:35,647
night.

114
00:03:35,687 --> 00:03:37,574
In the morning, his dog is scratching

115
00:03:37,614 --> 00:03:40,001
at the side door, and nobody comes to let

116
00:03:40,041 --> 00:03:41,108
it in.

117
00:03:41,148 --> 00:03:43,534
You and Mrs. Kowalski from next door break

118
00:03:43,574 --> 00:03:45,230
the side window and drag him out into

119
00:03:45,270 --> 00:03:46,336
the snow.

120
00:03:46,376 --> 00:03:48,035
Mr. Pratt makes it.

121
00:03:48,075 --> 00:03:49,827
He doesn't remember the night.

122
00:03:49,867 --> 00:03:51,394
He just keeps asking who was standing

123
00:03:51,434 --> 00:03:53,275
in the garage with him.

124
00:03:53,975 --> 00:03:55,143
Rule five.

125
00:03:55,183 --> 00:03:56,497
Stay dry.

126
00:03:56,537 --> 00:03:58,436
Cold doesn't kill you nearly as fast as

127
00:03:58,476 --> 00:03:59,311
wet.

128
00:03:59,351 --> 00:04:00,851
Wet socks.

129
00:04:00,891 --> 00:04:02,159
Wet gloves.

130
00:04:02,199 --> 00:04:03,599
A shirt you sweated through while

131
00:04:03,639 --> 00:04:04,601
shoveling.

132
00:04:04,641 --> 00:04:07,155
Wear layers, take one off before you start

133
00:04:07,195 --> 00:04:09,582
sweating, and change anything damp right

134
00:04:09,622 --> 00:04:10,619
away.

135
00:04:10,659 --> 00:04:12,639
Cover your head, your hands and your feet

136
00:04:12,679 --> 00:04:13,572
first.

137
00:04:13,612 --> 00:04:15,178
Cotton is the worst.

138
00:04:15,218 --> 00:04:16,738
Wool is the best.

139
00:04:16,778 --> 00:04:18,956
Your mom's old ski socks become the most

140
00:04:18,996 --> 00:04:21,161
valuable thing in the house.

141
00:04:21,861 --> 00:04:23,122
Rule six.

142
00:04:23,162 --> 00:04:24,728
Don't eat the snow.

143
00:04:24,768 --> 00:04:26,702
It feels like the obvious thing to do when

144
00:04:26,742 --> 00:04:28,099
the taps stop.

145
00:04:28,139 --> 00:04:29,573
But eating snow pulls heat out

146
00:04:29,613 --> 00:04:31,478
of your body, and you need every bit

147
00:04:31,518 --> 00:04:32,352
of it.

148
00:04:32,392 --> 00:04:33,846
Melt it first.

149
00:04:33,886 --> 00:04:36,122
Ten cups of snow make barely one cup

150
00:04:36,162 --> 00:04:38,200
of water, so melting it takes a lot

151
00:04:38,240 --> 00:04:39,446
of fuel.

152
00:04:39,486 --> 00:04:40,754
Then boil it.

153
00:04:40,794 --> 00:04:42,937
And there's another reason the card gives,

154
00:04:42,977 --> 00:04:45,053
in those same small letters.

155
00:04:45,093 --> 00:04:47,479
Some of the snow isn't snow.

156
00:04:48,330 --> 00:04:50,763
Day seven. The walkers.

157
00:04:51,062 --> 00:04:52,775
On the seventh day, the snow is above

158
00:04:52,815 --> 00:04:53,789
the windows.

159
00:04:53,829 --> 00:04:55,484
The only way out is through the upstairs

160
00:04:55,524 --> 00:04:57,207
window onto the drift.

161
00:04:57,247 --> 00:04:58,859
That's how you first see them.

162
00:04:58,899 --> 00:05:00,531
Out in the whiteout, between the buried

163
00:05:00,571 --> 00:05:01,533
houses.

164
00:05:01,573 --> 00:05:02,609
People.

165
00:05:02,649 --> 00:05:04,382
Walking slowly.

166
00:05:04,422 --> 00:05:06,251
Not wearing coats.

167
00:05:06,801 --> 00:05:08,181
Their clothes are clean.

168
00:05:08,221 --> 00:05:09,694
Their hair is dry.

169
00:05:09,734 --> 00:05:11,250
They never leave the paths between

170
00:05:11,290 --> 00:05:12,991
the houses, and they never look up

171
00:05:13,031 --> 00:05:14,272
at the windows.

172
00:05:14,312 --> 00:05:15,630
Not yet.

173
00:05:16,330 --> 00:05:17,638
Rule seven.

174
00:05:17,678 --> 00:05:19,774
If footprints lead to your door but not

175
00:05:19,814 --> 00:05:22,309
away from it, don't open the door.

176
00:05:22,349 --> 00:05:23,900
On the morning of day seven, there's

177
00:05:23,940 --> 00:05:26,330
a line of footprints in the fresh snow.

178
00:05:26,370 --> 00:05:28,308
They come from the middle of the street.

179
00:05:28,348 --> 00:05:30,099
Straight to your front door.

180
00:05:30,139 --> 00:05:31,547
And they stop.

181
00:05:31,587 --> 00:05:33,617
There's nobody on the porch.

182
00:05:33,657 --> 00:05:35,874
There are no footprints going back.

183
00:05:35,914 --> 00:05:37,929
Whatever made them is still somewhere

184
00:05:37,969 --> 00:05:39,987
between the road and your door.

185
00:05:40,027 --> 00:05:42,100
Keep the door shut until new snow covers

186
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:43,667
the tracks.

187
00:05:44,368 --> 00:05:45,629
Rule eight.

188
00:05:45,669 --> 00:05:48,628
After dark, keep every curtain closed.

189
00:05:48,668 --> 00:05:50,141
Not because of the cold.

190
00:05:50,181 --> 00:05:51,766
Because a lit window is the only thing

191
00:05:51,806 --> 00:05:53,837
you can see in a whiteout.

192
00:05:53,877 --> 00:05:56,279
And you're not the only one looking.

193
00:05:56,319 --> 00:05:58,520
Maya asks why the people outside don't get

194
00:05:58,560 --> 00:05:59,557
cold.

195
00:05:59,597 --> 00:06:01,380
You don't have an answer.

196
00:06:01,829 --> 00:06:03,391
On day eight, Maya peeks through

197
00:06:03,431 --> 00:06:04,556
the curtain.

198
00:06:04,596 --> 00:06:06,162
Just for a second.

199
00:06:06,202 --> 00:06:08,426
In the morning, every walker in the street

200
00:06:08,466 --> 00:06:12,396
is standing still, facing your house.

201
00:06:13,097 --> 00:06:14,358
Rule nine.

202
00:06:14,398 --> 00:06:16,517
Every hour, count everyone in the room out

203
00:06:16,557 --> 00:06:17,543
loud.

204
00:06:17,583 --> 00:06:18,619
One.

205
00:06:18,659 --> 00:06:19,834
Two.

206
00:06:19,874 --> 00:06:21,811
That's how it's supposed to go.

207
00:06:21,851 --> 00:06:23,866
On the ninth night, you count, and Maya

208
00:06:23,906 --> 00:06:26,761
counts, and you both say two.

209
00:06:26,801 --> 00:06:28,758
And then a third voice, from the dark

210
00:06:28,798 --> 00:06:31,618
corner by the stairs, says three.

211
00:06:31,658 --> 00:06:33,112
Count again.

212
00:06:33,152 --> 00:06:34,374
Out loud.

213
00:06:34,414 --> 00:06:36,208
Don't look at the corner until the number

214
00:06:36,248 --> 00:06:37,450
is right.

215
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:40,718
Day onetwo. The roof.

216
00:06:41,018 --> 00:06:42,975
On the twelfth day, the house starts

217
00:06:43,015 --> 00:06:44,164
to talk.

218
00:06:44,204 --> 00:06:45,565
Little cracks.

219
00:06:45,605 --> 00:06:47,960
Long, slow groans from the ceiling.

220
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:49,408
Snow is heavy.

221
00:06:49,448 --> 00:06:51,927
A foot of wet snow on a roof weighs as

222
00:06:51,967 --> 00:06:53,428
much as a car.

223
00:06:53,468 --> 00:06:55,529
And there are four feet.

224
00:06:56,230 --> 00:06:57,583
Rule ten.

225
00:06:57,623 --> 00:06:58,957
Listen to the roof.

226
00:06:58,997 --> 00:07:01,743
If it creaks, cracks, or the doors

227
00:07:01,783 --> 00:07:04,123
upstairs start sticking, the roof

228
00:07:04,163 --> 00:07:05,533
is bending.

229
00:07:05,573 --> 00:07:06,764
Get everyone into the room

230
00:07:06,804 --> 00:07:08,346
with the strongest walls.

231
00:07:08,386 --> 00:07:10,231
And don't stand in the middle of it.

232
00:07:10,271 --> 00:07:12,727
Two houses down, the Lees' garage roof

233
00:07:12,767 --> 00:07:14,763
gives way at three in the morning.

234
00:07:14,803 --> 00:07:16,508
It sounds like a gunshot.

235
00:07:16,548 --> 00:07:18,841
Then it sounds like nothing at all.

236
00:07:19,591 --> 00:07:20,852
Rule eleven.

237
00:07:20,892 --> 00:07:22,857
Never shovel alone.

238
00:07:22,897 --> 00:07:24,796
Shoveling heavy snow is one of the hardest

239
00:07:24,836 --> 00:07:26,506
things your heart can do.

240
00:07:26,546 --> 00:07:28,898
Every winter, people collapse in their own

241
00:07:28,938 --> 00:07:29,970
driveways.

242
00:07:30,010 --> 00:07:31,418
Take breaks.

243
00:07:31,458 --> 00:07:32,772
Lift less.

244
00:07:32,812 --> 00:07:34,781
Push the snow instead of lifting it, if

245
00:07:34,821 --> 00:07:35,911
you can.

246
00:07:35,951 --> 00:07:37,072
And have someone watching

247
00:07:37,112 --> 00:07:38,446
you from the window.

248
00:07:38,486 --> 00:07:40,547
Mrs. Kowalski from next door shovels

249
00:07:40,587 --> 00:07:42,513
a path between your houses.

250
00:07:42,553 --> 00:07:44,100
She's seventy-eight.

251
00:07:44,140 --> 00:07:46,495
She tells you she's done this before.

252
00:07:46,535 --> 00:07:47,823
She doesn't say when.

253
00:07:47,863 --> 00:07:50,590
Later, she shows you her own card.

254
00:07:50,630 --> 00:07:52,057
It's yellow and cracked.

255
00:07:52,097 --> 00:07:53,984
The date in the corner says February,

256
00:07:54,024 --> 00:07:55,613
1978.

257
00:07:55,653 --> 00:07:58,086
The handwriting looks like a child's.

258
00:07:58,836 --> 00:08:00,236
Rule twelve.

259
00:08:00,276 --> 00:08:02,384
Never sit in a running car that's buried

260
00:08:02,424 --> 00:08:03,468
in snow.

261
00:08:03,508 --> 00:08:05,546
When the snow covers the exhaust pipe,

262
00:08:05,586 --> 00:08:08,417
the fumes have nowhere to go but inside.

263
00:08:08,457 --> 00:08:10,379
If you're ever stuck in a car, run

264
00:08:10,419 --> 00:08:12,422
the engine ten minutes an hour, keep

265
00:08:12,462 --> 00:08:14,733
a window open a crack, and keep the pipe

266
00:08:14,773 --> 00:08:15,921
clear.

267
00:08:15,961 --> 00:08:18,406
On day twelve, the Garcias try to warm up

268
00:08:18,446 --> 00:08:19,571
in their van.

269
00:08:19,611 --> 00:08:22,013
Mrs. Kowalski finds them in time.

270
00:08:22,053 --> 00:08:23,185
Barely.

271
00:08:23,635 --> 00:08:26,501
Quick question before day twenty.

272
00:08:26,541 --> 00:08:28,161
Which of these rules do you think you'd

273
00:08:28,201 --> 00:08:29,694
forget first?

274
00:08:29,734 --> 00:08:31,439
Tell me in the comments.

275
00:08:31,479 --> 00:08:33,308
And stay warm.

276
00:08:34,058 --> 00:08:37,327
Day two0. The lights.

277
00:08:37,627 --> 00:08:39,677
On the twentieth day, the walkers start

278
00:08:39,717 --> 00:08:41,050
carrying lights.

279
00:08:41,090 --> 00:08:43,372
That same week, the radio crackles back

280
00:08:43,412 --> 00:08:45,210
to life for one hour.

281
00:08:45,250 --> 00:08:47,776
Your dad's voice, really his, reading

282
00:08:47,816 --> 00:08:50,160
a list of names from the hospital.

283
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:51,488
Yours is on it.

284
00:08:51,528 --> 00:08:52,749
Safe.

285
00:08:52,789 --> 00:08:54,014
Waiting.

286
00:08:54,715 --> 00:08:56,161
Rule thirteen.

287
00:08:56,201 --> 00:08:58,046
Never follow a light in the whiteout.

288
00:08:58,086 --> 00:09:00,182
In a real whiteout, you can't tell up

289
00:09:00,222 --> 00:09:01,463
from down.

290
00:09:01,503 --> 00:09:03,390
People get lost ten steps from their own

291
00:09:03,430 --> 00:09:04,555
front door.

292
00:09:04,595 --> 00:09:06,262
So when you see a warm yellow light out

293
00:09:06,302 --> 00:09:08,576
there, it feels like rescue.

294
00:09:08,616 --> 00:09:10,368
It moves when you move.

295
00:09:10,408 --> 00:09:12,114
It stops when you stop.

296
00:09:12,154 --> 00:09:14,509
It's always just a little farther away.

297
00:09:14,549 --> 00:09:16,483
If you ever have to go out in a whiteout,

298
00:09:16,523 --> 00:09:18,410
tie a rope to the door and never let go

299
00:09:18,450 --> 00:09:19,505
of it.

300
00:09:19,545 --> 00:09:22,222
Mrs. Kowalski's dog, Bear, followed one

301
00:09:22,262 --> 00:09:23,619
on day twenty.

302
00:09:23,659 --> 00:09:25,535
In the spring, they find his collar

303
00:09:25,575 --> 00:09:27,346
on the roof of the school.

304
00:09:28,046 --> 00:09:29,585
Rule fourteen.

305
00:09:29,625 --> 00:09:31,981
Never answer a voice from the roof.

306
00:09:32,021 --> 00:09:33,931
At night, something walks on the snow

307
00:09:33,971 --> 00:09:35,212
above your ceiling.

308
00:09:35,252 --> 00:09:36,799
Slow steps.

309
00:09:36,839 --> 00:09:38,587
Then it lies down, right over

310
00:09:38,627 --> 00:09:40,654
your mattress, and it whispers your name

311
00:09:40,694 --> 00:09:42,028
through the shingles.

312
00:09:42,068 --> 00:09:43,819
In your dad's voice.

313
00:09:43,859 --> 00:09:46,095
Your dad is at the hospital, forty miles

314
00:09:46,135 --> 00:09:46,819
away.

315
00:09:46,859 --> 00:09:47,941
You know that.

316
00:09:47,981 --> 00:09:49,973
You put your hands over Maya's ears until

317
00:09:50,013 --> 00:09:51,110
the morning.

318
00:09:51,810 --> 00:09:53,118
Rule fifteen.

319
00:09:53,158 --> 00:09:55,173
Hang something bright on your door, so

320
00:09:55,213 --> 00:09:57,046
rescuers can find the house.

321
00:09:57,086 --> 00:09:58,540
A red towel.

322
00:09:58,580 --> 00:10:00,126
An orange vest.

323
00:10:00,166 --> 00:10:02,383
Anything that doesn't look like snow.

324
00:10:02,423 --> 00:10:04,217
You hang Maya's red sled on the door

325
00:10:04,257 --> 00:10:05,336
handle.

326
00:10:05,376 --> 00:10:07,333
By morning, someone has hung a second one

327
00:10:07,373 --> 00:10:08,474
next to it.

328
00:10:08,514 --> 00:10:10,482
You don't own a second one.

329
00:10:11,332 --> 00:10:13,532
Day three0. The knock.

330
00:10:13,833 --> 00:10:16,746
By day thirty, the food is running low.

331
00:10:16,786 --> 00:10:18,453
You've burned the coffee table, two

332
00:10:18,493 --> 00:10:20,534
chairs, and a bookshelf.

333
00:10:20,574 --> 00:10:22,450
And Maya has stopped asking when Dad

334
00:10:22,490 --> 00:10:23,812
is coming home.

335
00:10:23,852 --> 00:10:25,774
On the night of day thirty, someone

336
00:10:25,814 --> 00:10:27,213
knocks.

337
00:10:27,914 --> 00:10:29,128
Rule sixteen.

338
00:10:29,168 --> 00:10:30,881
If someone knocks and there's no snow

339
00:10:30,921 --> 00:10:33,428
on their shoulders, don't open the door.

340
00:10:33,468 --> 00:10:35,498
You look through the gap in the curtain.

341
00:10:35,538 --> 00:10:36,853
It's Dad.

342
00:10:36,893 --> 00:10:38,440
His hospital coat.

343
00:10:38,480 --> 00:10:39,934
His glasses.

344
00:10:39,974 --> 00:10:41,428
He's smiling.

345
00:10:41,468 --> 00:10:43,158
He's been walking through a blizzard

346
00:10:43,198 --> 00:10:44,891
for forty miles.

347
00:10:44,931 --> 00:10:46,691
And there is not a single flake of snow

348
00:10:46,731 --> 00:10:47,875
on him.

349
00:10:48,725 --> 00:10:50,318
He knocks again.

350
00:10:50,358 --> 00:10:52,733
Then he says, open up, kiddo, it's cold

351
00:10:52,773 --> 00:10:53,828
out here.

352
00:10:53,868 --> 00:10:55,647
He's not shivering.

353
00:10:55,687 --> 00:10:57,702
His breath doesn't steam.

354
00:10:58,352 --> 00:11:00,038
Close the curtain.

355
00:11:00,078 --> 00:11:02,480
Sit with your back against the door.

356
00:11:02,520 --> 00:11:04,829
And wait for the knocking to stop.

357
00:11:04,869 --> 00:11:06,768
Maya wakes up and starts walking toward

358
00:11:06,808 --> 00:11:09,175
the door, because it's Dad.

359
00:11:09,215 --> 00:11:11,849
You hold her so tight that she cries.

360
00:11:11,889 --> 00:11:13,440
In the morning, there are footprints

361
00:11:13,480 --> 00:11:14,616
on the porch.

362
00:11:14,656 --> 00:11:16,110
Leading away.

363
00:11:16,150 --> 00:11:17,840
For the first time.

364
00:11:18,540 --> 00:11:19,940
Rule seventeen.

365
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:21,546
Eat before you're hungry.

366
00:11:21,586 --> 00:11:23,942
Your body burns food to make heat.

367
00:11:23,982 --> 00:11:25,614
Skipping meals to make the food last

368
00:11:25,654 --> 00:11:28,354
longer makes you colder, slower, and more

369
00:11:28,394 --> 00:11:30,331
likely to make mistakes.

370
00:11:30,371 --> 00:11:31,593
Small meals.

371
00:11:31,633 --> 00:11:32,669
Often.

372
00:11:32,709 --> 00:11:34,786
Hot drinks whenever you can.

373
00:11:34,826 --> 00:11:37,189
You split the last can of soup with Maya,

374
00:11:37,229 --> 00:11:39,674
and you let her have the bigger half.

375
00:11:40,373 --> 00:11:41,866
Rule eighteen.

376
00:11:41,906 --> 00:11:43,658
Never burn the card.

377
00:11:43,698 --> 00:11:45,783
By day thirty-two, there's nothing left

378
00:11:45,823 --> 00:11:47,586
in the living room to burn.

379
00:11:47,626 --> 00:11:49,258
You look at the white plastic card

380
00:11:49,298 --> 00:11:51,189
on the fridge for a long time.

381
00:11:51,229 --> 00:11:53,445
Then you burn your homework instead.

382
00:11:53,485 --> 00:11:55,655
Every notebook since second grade.

383
00:11:55,695 --> 00:11:57,780
Mrs. Kowalski burned hers on day

384
00:11:57,820 --> 00:11:59,165
twenty-nine.

385
00:11:59,205 --> 00:12:01,793
On day thirty, her house was dark.

386
00:12:01,833 --> 00:12:03,952
On day thirty-one, her porch had a second

387
00:12:03,992 --> 00:12:05,396
set of footprints.

388
00:12:05,436 --> 00:12:07,033
Only going in.

389
00:12:07,883 --> 00:12:11,105
Day four0. The last snow.

390
00:12:11,405 --> 00:12:13,234
On the fortieth morning, you wake up

391
00:12:13,274 --> 00:12:15,618
to a sound you forgot existed.

392
00:12:15,658 --> 00:12:16,694
Dripping.

393
00:12:16,734 --> 00:12:18,021
The snow has stopped.

394
00:12:18,061 --> 00:12:19,535
The sky is blue.

395
00:12:19,575 --> 00:12:22,704
And it's so bright outside that it hurts.

396
00:12:23,404 --> 00:12:24,758
Rule nineteen.

397
00:12:24,798 --> 00:12:27,103
When the snow stops, don't go outside

398
00:12:27,143 --> 00:12:28,872
until you hear birds.

399
00:12:28,912 --> 00:12:30,497
The first quiet morning feels like

400
00:12:30,537 --> 00:12:31,546
the end.

401
00:12:31,586 --> 00:12:33,972
Your neighbors are already outside, waving

402
00:12:34,012 --> 00:12:35,567
at you from the drifts.

403
00:12:35,607 --> 00:12:37,014
All of them.

404
00:12:37,054 --> 00:12:38,555
Even the Pratts.

405
00:12:38,595 --> 00:12:40,373
Even Mrs. Kowalski.

406
00:12:40,413 --> 00:12:41,728
Even Bear.

407
00:12:41,768 --> 00:12:43,613
None of them are wearing coats.

408
00:12:43,653 --> 00:12:45,389
There are no birds.

409
00:12:46,039 --> 00:12:47,651
You keep the door shut.

410
00:12:47,691 --> 00:12:48,820
You wait.

411
00:12:48,860 --> 00:12:51,490
At noon, a crow lands on the power line

412
00:12:51,530 --> 00:12:52,795
and screams.

413
00:12:52,835 --> 00:12:54,548
And when you look back down, the street

414
00:12:54,588 --> 00:12:55,562
is empty.

415
00:12:55,602 --> 00:12:56,591
No people.

416
00:12:56,631 --> 00:12:57,992
No footprints.

417
00:12:58,032 --> 00:12:59,768
Just snow.

418
00:13:00,318 --> 00:13:02,813
Mrs. Kowalski's house is still dark.

419
00:13:02,853 --> 00:13:04,636
Her front door is open.

420
00:13:05,286 --> 00:13:07,781
At sunset, you hear the plows.

421
00:13:07,821 --> 00:13:09,553
Then a helicopter.

422
00:13:09,593 --> 00:13:11,469
Then, finally, your dad, wearing a coat

423
00:13:11,509 --> 00:13:13,489
covered in snow, banging on the door

424
00:13:13,529 --> 00:13:14,828
and crying.

425
00:13:14,868 --> 00:13:16,941
Later, while the soldiers carry Maya

426
00:13:16,981 --> 00:13:19,159
to the truck, you go to the shed for her

427
00:13:19,199 --> 00:13:20,149
sled.

428
00:13:20,189 --> 00:13:22,638
And on the workbench, there's a box.

429
00:13:22,678 --> 00:13:24,503
White plastic cards.

430
00:13:24,543 --> 00:13:25,625
Hundreds of them.

431
00:13:25,665 --> 00:13:26,608
Blank.

432
00:13:26,648 --> 00:13:28,261
And a black marker.

433
00:13:28,301 --> 00:13:29,666
Half used.

434
00:13:30,415 --> 00:13:32,523
You take the card off your fridge and look

435
00:13:32,563 --> 00:13:34,636
at the handwriting closely, for the first

436
00:13:34,676 --> 00:13:35,697
time.

437
00:13:35,737 --> 00:13:37,055
It's yours.

438
00:13:38,005 --> 00:13:39,405
The last rule.

439
00:13:39,445 --> 00:13:42,203
Write the rules for whoever comes next.

440
00:13:43,053 --> 00:13:45,269
So, here's the thing about those cards.

441
00:13:45,309 --> 00:13:47,904
Every winter, somewhere, one of them shows

442
00:13:47,944 --> 00:13:49,290
up on a door.

443
00:13:49,330 --> 00:13:52,429
And somebody, somewhere, has to write it.

444
00:13:52,469 --> 00:13:53,783
Twenty rules.

445
00:13:53,823 --> 00:13:55,211
Some of them could save your life this

446
00:13:55,251 --> 00:13:56,550
winter, for real.

447
00:13:56,590 --> 00:13:58,091
Fill the bathtub.

448
00:13:58,131 --> 00:13:59,631
Clear the vents.

449
00:13:59,671 --> 00:14:01,609
Never burn anything indoors.

450
00:14:01,649 --> 00:14:03,803
The rest, well, I hope you never need

451
00:14:03,843 --> 00:14:04,515
them.

452
00:14:04,555 --> 00:14:06,164
But if you ever look out the window during

453
00:14:06,204 --> 00:14:08,161
a storm, and someone's standing there

454
00:14:08,201 --> 00:14:09,879
with no snow on their shoulders,

455
00:14:10,012 --> 00:14:12,019
you already know what to do.

456
00:14:12,059 --> 00:14:13,853
And if you were writing the card, what

457
00:14:13,893 --> 00:14:16,186
would rule twenty-one be?

458
00:14:16,226 --> 00:14:17,932
Tell me in the comments.

459
00:14:17,972 --> 00:14:19,087
I'll be reading them.
